window.docContents[60].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-colouring-box-products":{"contents":"We can restrict a colouring C of G □ H, along any fixed vertex of one factor, to\na colouring of the other factor with the same colours: v ↦ C (v, w₀) colours G\nand w ↦ C (v₀, w) colours H.\n\nThe colouring of G induced by a colouring of G □ H./-- The colouring of G induced by a colouring of G □ H. -/\ndef Coloring.ofBoxProdLeft {γ : Type*} (C : (G □ H).Coloring γ) (w : W) :\n    G.Coloring γ :=\n  C.comp <| boxProdLeft G H w\n\n\nThe colouring of H induced by a colouring of G □ H./-- The colouring of H induced by a colouring of G □ H. -/\ndef Coloring.ofBoxProdRight {γ : Type*} (C : (G □ H).Coloring γ) (v : V) :\n    H.Coloring γ :=\n  C.comp <| boxProdRight G H v\n\n\nConversely, given k-colourings of G and H we can k-colour G □ H as follows.\nIf C₁ : G.Coloring (Fin k) and C₂ : H.Coloring (Fin k) then C : V × W → Fin k\ndefined by C (v, w) = C₁ v + C₂ w, with addition modulo k in Fin k, is a valid\ncolouring of G □ H. Along an edge one coordinate is fixed, so the colours differ\nbecause the colouring of the other factor is valid.\n\nThe only property of addition this uses is cancellation: a + b = a + c implies\nb = c, and b + a = c + a implies b = c (IsCancelAdd). So we define the\ncolouring for any colour type γ with a cancellative addition. Mathlib has\nIsCancelAdd (Fin k) for every k, so this covers Fin k. Asking for an\nadditive group instead would be too much: Fin k is an additive group only when\nk ≠ 0.\n\nThe colouring (v, w) ↦ C₁ v + C₂ w of G □ H induced by colourings C₁ of G\nand C₂ of H, for colours with a cancellative addition./-- The colouring (v, w) ↦ C₁ v + C₂ w of G □ H induced by colourings C₁ of G\nand C₂ of H, for colours with a cancellative addition. -/\ndef Coloring.boxProd {γ : Type*} [Add γ] [IsCancelAdd γ] (C₁ : G.Coloring γ)\n    (C₂ : H.Coloring γ) : (G □ H).Coloring γ :=\n  Coloring.mk (fun x => C₁ x.1 + C₂ x.2) <| by\n    rintro ⟨v, w⟩ ⟨v', w'⟩ (⟨hadj, rfl⟩ | ⟨hadj, rfl⟩)\n    · simpa using C₁.valid hadj\n    · simpa using C₂.valid hadj\n\n\nIf G and H are both k-colourable then so is G □ H.\n\ntheorem Colorable.boxProd {k : ℕ} (h₁ : G.Colorable k) (h₂ : H.Colorable k) :\n    (G □ H).Colorable k :=\n  ⟨Coloring.boxProd h₁.some h₂.some⟩\n\n\nCombining this with the restricted colourings above, if G and H are both nonempty\nthen G □ H is k-colourable iff both G and H are. (Nonemptiness is needed\nhere: if H has no vertices then neither does G □ H, so G □ H is colourable even\nwhen G is not.)\n\ntheorem colorable_boxProd_iff [Nonempty V] [Nonempty W] {k : ℕ} :\n    (G □ H).Colorable k ↔ G.Colorable k ∧ H.Colorable k :=\n  ⟨fun ⟨C⟩ => ⟨⟨Coloring.ofBoxProdLeft C (Classical.arbitrary W)⟩,\n      ⟨Coloring.ofBoxProdRight C (Classical.arbitrary V)⟩⟩,\n   fun h => h.1.boxProd h.2⟩\n\n\nHence if G and H are both nonempty then the chromatic number of G □ H is the\nmaximum of the chromatic numbers of G and H.\n\nTo prove an equality a = b in ℕ∞ it suffices to show that b ≤ m ↔ a ≤ m for all\nm (eq_of_forall_ge_iff). We then split into the cases m = ⊤, which is trivial,\nand m a natural number, where chromaticNumber_le_iff_colorable turns both sides\ninto colourability statements.\n\ntheorem chromaticNumber_boxProd [Nonempty V] [Nonempty W] :\n    χ (G □ H) = max (χ G) (χ H) := by\n  refine eq_of_forall_ge_iff fun m => ?_\n  cases m with\n  | top => simp\n  | coe m => simp [chromaticNumber_le_iff_colorable, colorable_boxProd_iff]\n\n\nMathlib has the same result for the disjoint union G ⊕g H, where no nonemptiness\nassumption is needed.\n\n#check chromaticNumber_sum -- χ (G ⊕g H) = max (χ G) (χ H)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Colouring Concrete Graphs","header":"7.5.2. Colouring Box Products","id":"/Colorings/#section-colouring-box-products"}});